# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# tests/proprietary-identifiers.example
#
# Format reference for the operator's own forbidden values.
#
# This repository is published, so it deliberately contains none of the values it
# must be protected against: a scanner that ships the account ids, distribution
# ids, hostnames and organisation names it forbids publishes exactly what it is
# meant to protect. Supply the list out-of-band instead, one entry per line, in
# `<label>|<extended-regex>` form:
#
#   * In CI, set the PROPRIETARY_IDENTIFIERS repository secret
#     (Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions) to the list. It is encrypted
#     at rest and never committed.
#
#   * Locally, copy this file to tests/proprietary-identifiers.local, which is
#     git-ignored, and fill it in.
#
# Then `tests/verify-no-identifiers.sh` fails when any listed value appears in the
# tree. Matched values are reported as <redacted> so a public CI log cannot become
# the leak, and REQUIRE_PROPRIETARY_LIST=1 turns a missing list into a hard
# failure (CI sets that wherever the secret exists).
#
# Rules: blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored. An entry with no `|`
# is treated as a literal string to look for. Documentation placeholders
# (000000000000, 111122223333, 123456789012, EXAMPLE, PLACEHOLDER, REDACTED,
# your-, dummy, CHANGEME) are allowlisted for the generic credential/ARN shapes
# only — a value you list yourself is always enforced, with no exceptions.
#
# The examples are commented out on purpose: a live entry here would have to be a
# placeholder, and the template file itself contains it, which would produce a
# finding against this file the moment you copied it. Uncomment and replace with
# your own values in tests/proprietary-identifiers.local — never in this file.
#
#   proprietary-account-id|<your 12-digit account id>
#   proprietary-org|<the github org or user that owns your deploys>
#   proprietary-repo|<an internal repository name>
#   proprietary-distribution|<a cloudfront distribution id>
#   proprietary-host|<a hostname you operate, dots escaped: example\.com>
#   proprietary-project|<an internal project name>
#   proprietary-bucket|<an s3 bucket name that is not public knowledge>
#
# Accepts extended regex, so several values can share one line:
#
#   proprietary-distribution|DISTIDONE|DISTIDTWO|DISTIDTHREE
#   proprietary-host|example\.com|example\.internal
